1: # P3K.48. The ordinary marked plumbing product and its circle-axis defect
2: 
3: Working proof for audit. In the actual selected-cusp family of
4: P3F.54/P3F.56, the labelled three-curve plumbing does have a periodic
5: orientation-preserving ordinary smooth product. We construct it below.
6: It preserves the three labelled curves and both labelled vertices.
7: It is based at the given initial fibre. It is not a delta-equivariant
8: product: after the extending unit maps, its fixed-curve normal circle
9: has the explicit varying axis below, of degree \(+3\) on the indicated
10: collapsed cylinder. This gives the precise local circle defect, without
11: assigning a common-exterior variation matrix, nonfixed relative integer,
12: or other-fan return.
13: 
14: ## OP.1. One actual vertex product and all three normal transition loops
15: 
16: Use PO.1 of P3K.47 and its ordered charts \((a,b,c)\), \((u,v,w)\).
17: Keep the first vertex coordinates and use at the second vertex the
18: actual unit map
19: \[
20:                   (u,v,w)\longmapsto(u,\eta v,\kappa w).
21:                                                                \tag{OP.1}
22: \]
23: Here the units are first restricted to the curves, hence to \(q=0\).
24: The higher terms of their original chart maps are restored below.
25: The reference vertex at \(\rho_*\) uses the same map with its initial
26: constant units. This makes (OP.1) an actual based vertex comparison,
27: rather than a replacement of the initial coordinate system.
28: 
29: The tangent coordinates on each pair of curve charts now have literal
30: reciprocal transition. With the following ordered complex normal
31: coordinates the three transitions are
32: \[
33:  \begin{array}{c|c|c|c}
34:   &z&\text{first normal pair}&\text{second normal pair}\\ \hline
35:  C_0&b&(a,c)&(\eta v,u)\\
36:  C_1&c&(a,b)&(\kappa w,u)\\
37:  C_2&a&(b,c)&(\eta v,\kappa w).
38:  \end{array}
39: \]
40: Their normal transition matrices are \(zD_i(\rho)\), where
41: \[
42:  D_0=\operatorname{diag}(\eta/\alpha,\kappa\alpha),\quad
43:  D_1=\operatorname{diag}(\kappa/g,g\eta),\quad
44:  D_2=\operatorname{diag}(\eta,\kappa).                 \tag{OP.2}
45: \]
46: These are direct substitutions in the three rows of PO.1. Put
47: \(K_i=D_i(\rho_*)^{-1}D_i(\rho)\). In particular
48: \[
49:  \begin{aligned}
50:  K_0&=\operatorname{diag}
51:    ((\eta/\eta_*)(\alpha_*/\alpha),
52:              (\kappa/\kappa_*)(\alpha/\alpha_*)),\\
53:  K_1&=\operatorname{diag}
54:    ((\kappa/\kappa_*)(g_*/g),(g/g_*)(\eta/\eta_*)),\\
55:  K_2&=\operatorname{diag}(\eta/\eta_*,\kappa/\kappa_*).
56:  \end{aligned}                                               \tag{OP.3}
57: \]
58: Each determinant has winding \(-6\), and each \(K_i\) is the identity
59: at the initial parameter. Thus their real oriented normal loops have
60: even parity. We will not rely on parity alone: OP.2 gives their
61: explicit contractions.
62: 
63: Write the rho circle as \(\rho=\rho_*e^{i\theta}\). Each unit of
64: winding zero in (OP.3) has its actual periodic logarithm, chosen zero
65: at \(\theta=0\). For \(\kappa/\kappa_*\), retain the factor
66: \(e^{-6i\theta}\), and take the periodic logarithm of the remaining
67: unit. Multiplication by the exponentials of scalar multiples of these
68: logarithms supplies exact based homotopies of the unit factors. After
69: these carried unit maps, the remaining real normal loops are
70: \[
71:  K_0^{\rm ang}=K_2^{\rm ang}
72:         =\operatorname{diag}(1,e^{-6i\theta}),\qquad
73:  K_1^{\rm ang}=\operatorname{diag}(e^{-6i\theta},1).
74:                                                                \tag{OP.4}
75: \]
76: The same logarithms can be inserted in the final maps, as explained
77: in OP.3; the original units are not discarded.
78: 
79: ## OP.2. Explicit based quaternion contractions in the actual order
80: 
81: For an ordered pair of complex normal coordinates \((n_0,n_1)\),
82: we use exactly the K23 convention
83: \[
84:                          Q=n_0+j\overline{n_1}.       \tag{OP.5}
85: \]
86: This real coordinate has the original complex normal orientation.
87: Choose a smooth function \(\vartheta:[0,1]\to[0,\pi/2]\), constant
88: near both ends and strictly increasing between them, and put
89: \[
90:  \begin{aligned}
91:  p(s,\theta)&=\sin\vartheta(s)\,e^{-3i\theta}
92:                                       +j\cos\vartheta(s),\\
93:  H(s,\theta)&=p(s,\theta)p(s,0)^{-1}.
94:  \end{aligned}                                               \tag{OP.6}
95: \]
96: Both factors are unit quaternions. The maps are smooth and periodic,
97: and satisfy
98: \[
99:  H(0,\theta)=1,\qquad H(1,\theta)=e^{-3i\theta},\qquad
100:  H(s,0)=H(s,2\pi)=1.                                  \tag{OP.7}
101: \]
102: Define two actual oriented real normal maps
103: \[
104:     T_-(s,\theta)(Q)=H QH^{-1},\qquad
105:     T_+(s,\theta)(Q)=H QH.                            \tag{OP.8}
106: \]
107: Their inverses are displayed by replacing the two factors with their
108: inverses in reverse order. They are orthogonal and orientation
109: preserving. At \(s=1\), the first sends the quaternion coefficients
110: \((n_0,\overline{n_1})\) to
111: \((n_0,e^{6i\theta}\overline{n_1})\). Thus in the original complex
112: coordinates it is
113: \((n_0,n_1)\mapsto(n_0,e^{-6i\theta}n_1)\).
114: The second is \((n_0,n_1)\mapsto(e^{-6i\theta}n_0,n_1)\).
115: These are precisely the two loops in (OP.4). In particular no
116: unconjugated quaternion coordinate is substituted for (OP.5).
117: 
118: We use \(T_-\) for \(C_0,C_2\) and \(T_+\) for \(C_1\).
119: Multiplying these normal maps by the diagonal exponentials of the
120: actual periodic unit logarithms, with a cutoff in \(s\), gives a
121: contraction \(\mathcal K_i(s,\theta)\) of each full loop (OP.3).
122: The cutoff is zero near \(s=0\) and one near \(s=1\), so
123: \[
124:  \mathcal K_i(0,\theta)=I,\quad
125:  \mathcal K_i(1,\theta)=K_i(\theta),\quad
126:  \mathcal K_i(s,0)=\mathcal K_i(s,2\pi)=I.             \tag{OP.9}
127: \]
128: All maps are invertible throughout. The diagonal unit homotopies
129: commute with the physical circle; the explicitly displayed maps
130: \(T_\pm\) need not do so.
131: 
132: ## OP.3. Gluing the actual ordinary plumbing product
133: 
134: Form the marked regular neighbourhood from two small actual coordinate
135: balls at the vertices and three disjoint normal tubes along the curve
136: interiors. On each curve choose a radial coordinate \(s=s(|z|)\)
137: which is zero near its first vertex and one near its second. In the
138: first normal frame send
139: \[
140:                     (z,n)\longmapsto
141:                        (z,\mathcal K_i(s(|z|),\theta)n).
142:                                                                \tag{OP.10}
143: \]
144: It is the identity near the first vertex. Near the second it is
145: \(K_i\), and the equality
146: \[
147:                zD_i(\rho_*)K_i=zD_i(\rho)            \tag{OP.11}
148: \]
149: shows that it is exactly the second vertex comparison (OP.1).
150: Consequently these three maps and the two actual vertex maps agree
151: on their common collars. There is no unresolved corner: the open
152: curve tubes are disjoint away from the vertex balls, and (OP.11)
153: holds in the ordered normal directions of each incident axis.
154: 
155: The maps on the actual coordinate germs have the same first normal
156: derivatives as these displayed bundle maps. The remaining higher
157: terms are removed on smaller tube collars by normal rescaling.
158: For a map \(F\) preserving the zero section, put
159: \(R_t(z,n)=(z,tn)\) and use the literal conjugation
160: \(R_t^{-1}FR_t\) for \(t>0\). Its limit consists of the given
161: zero-section map and its invertible normal derivative. Only the
162: normal vector is scaled in this construction. On the compact parameter circle
163: one common sufficiently small tube makes this a smooth isotopy of
164: invertible germs through \(t=0\). On each vertex collar the original
165: unit maps have nonzero diagonal derivative and are treated by the
166: same rescaling, with the vertex coordinate balls protected.
167: 
168: For precision about gluing this isotopy, take its velocity
169: \((\partial_tF_t)F_t^{-1}\), multiply it by a smooth collar cutoff,
170: and integrate. This supplies the actual collar diffeomorphism
171: between the original germ and the displayed normal map. The cutoffs
172: are chosen on the three disjoint tube interiors and agree with the
173: already fixed vertex maps on their protected collars. Thus they
174: glue without changing any other incident axis. All isotopies are
175: periodic and are the identity at the reference parameter.
176: 
177: Finally transport the rounded boundary and use the resulting collar
178: isotopy to fix one regular-neighbourhood radius. This constructs a
179: periodic smooth product of the actual labelled plumbing with the
180: reference plumbing. The vertex maps are complex-unit maps, the
181: curve maps preserve their complex orientations, and the normal maps
182: are in the positive real group. Hence the product preserves the
183: six-manifold orientation. It is an ordinary smooth product of the
184: labelled neighbourhood; no source-height-preserving or
185: delta-equivariant product is asserted.
186: 
187: ## OP.4. The physical circle and the exact degree-three axis
188: 
189: We now retain the physical circle in this product. After the actual
190: extending unit maps of OP.1, on the fixed-curve normal quaternion
191: (OP.5) it is \(Q\mapsto Qe^{-i\phi}\). Conjugating by the actual
192: map \(T_-\) gives
193: \[
194:        Q\longmapsto Q\,H(s,\theta)e^{-i\phi}H(s,\theta)^{-1}.
195:                                                                \tag{OP.12}
196: \]
197: The same right-hand axis occurs after multiplication by the fixed
198: source Hopf representative used to form \(q_H\). Thus the circle
199: axis in the ordinary product is
200: \[
201:                          \nu(s,\theta)=H iH^{-1}.     \tag{OP.13}
202: \]
203: It equals \(i\) on both ends of the cylinder, but it is not the
204: constant axis on its interior. The zero-winding diagonal unit maps
205: are the previously displayed transported maps; contracting their
206: logarithms carries (OP.12) to the formula written here and leaves
207: the following integer degree unchanged.
208: 
209: Orient the cylinder by \(ds\wedge d\theta\), collapse each boundary
210: circle to its endpoint, and orient the target sphere of imaginary
211: unit quaternions by its outward normal. We compute the degree rather
212: than infer it from parity. Put \(A=\sin\vartheta\),
213: \(B=\cos\vartheta\), \(C=\cos3\theta\), \(S=\sin3\theta\).
214: Then (OP.6) is literally
215: \[
216:  H=(B^2+A^2C)-A^2S,i+AB(1-C),j+AB S,k.            \tag{OP.14}
217: \]
218: The equation \(HiH^{-1}=-i\) is equivalent to zero real and
219: \(i\)-coefficients of \(H\). Its only solutions are
220: \[
221:  \vartheta=\pi/4,\qquad
222:  \theta=(\pi+2\pi j)/3,
223:                    \quad j=0,1,2.                    \tag{OP.15}
224: \]
225: At each one \(H=j\), and direct differentiation gives
226: \[
227:                     \partial_s\nu=4\vartheta'(s)k,
228:                     \partial_\theta\nu=3j.           \tag{OP.16}
229: \]
230: The ordered basis \((k,j)\) is positive at the target point \(-i\),
231: since \((-i,k,j)\) has the ambient orientation \((i,j,k)\).
232: Every preimage in (OP.15) is therefore regular and positive. Hence
233: \[
234:                               \deg\nu=+3.             \tag{OP.17}
235: \]
236: 
237: For completeness the two nonfixed normal actions are also explicit.
238: In their ordered normal coordinates of OP.1, the initial actions are
239: \[
240:  R_1(\phi)Q=e^{-i\phi/2}Qe^{-i\phi/2},\qquad
241:  R_2(\phi)Q=e^{i\phi/2}Qe^{-i\phi/2}.
242: \]
243: Their base coordinates have weights \(+1,-1\), respectively.
244: The ordinary product carries their normal actions to
245: \[
246:  \begin{aligned}
247:  C_1:quad Q&\longmapsto
248:  (He^{-i\phi/2}H^{-1})Q(H^{-1}e^{-i\phi/2}H),\\
249:  C_2:quad Q&\longmapsto
250:  (He^{i\phi/2}H^{-1})Q(He^{-i\phi/2}H^{-1}).
251:  \end{aligned}                                               \tag{OP.18}
252: \]
253: The paired central signs make both formulas close at \(\phi=2\pi\).
254: Together with their base rotations and the unit maps already retained,
255: these are the actual transported circle actions on all three tubes.
256: They agree with the vertex action on the protected collars.
257: 
258: The product in OP.3 thus provides a possible ordinary common boundary
259: marking, with the nonconstant circle action (OP.12)--(OP.18). The
260: circle-trace and stable-derivative arguments for a constant equivariant
261: product require a further correction in this marking. That correction
262: is not evaluated here. In particular neither a rank-one initial
263: variation matrix nor vanishing nonfixed relative derivatives follows
264: from (OP.17).
265: 
266: The exact normal-matrix, quaternion and degree checks are in
267: code/part3/source_cusp_ordinary_product.py.
